\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The recorded claim}
Stage 1 of the elliptic curve method of factorization is controlled by a single parameter, the smoothness bound $B_1$. A claim in circulation records a hard upper limit on that parameter:
\begin{quote}
when $B_1 \gtrsim \min(p,q)$, every Hasse-window order divides $\lcm(1..B_1)$, all curves degenerate simultaneously, uncapped $\E[T]$ infinite,
\end{quote}
together with a validity edge $B_1 \lesssim \min(p,q)/2$.

The claim has an unusual shape. Most parameter sweeps saturate; this one asserts a \emph{cliff}, a point past which additional effort destroys the method. Cliffs in a monotone computational resource are rare enough to warrant scrutiny.

\subsection{What we prove}
The mechanism in the recorded sentence is right; the outcome label attached to it is not. We prove: universal \emph{success} at the wall (\S\ref{sec:size}--\S\ref{sec:outcome}); that $\E[T]$ is never infinite and is antitone in $B$ (\S\ref{sec:expected}); that a wall is not a possible shape for the firing ledger, since firing is monotone (\S\ref{sec:mono}); that the genuine destruction threshold is $\max(p,q)$, with exact block counts and an explicit witness (\S\ref{sec:counts}); and that the recorded sentence is the exact image of one non-injective step in an outcome ledger (\S\ref{sec:ledger}). \S\ref{sec:experiment} reports a 600-trial corroborating sweep with full disclosures.

\section{Setup and definitions}

Throughout, $N = pq$ with $p \neq q$ prime, and $p < q$ unless stated otherwise.

\begin{definition}[Powersmoothness]
For $B,n \in \N$, $n$ is \emph{$B$-powersmooth} if every prime power $\ell^{e}$ exactly dividing $n$ satisfies $\ell^{e} \le B$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Stage-1 scalar]\label{def:scalar}
The stage-1 scalar $k(B)$ is the product over primes $\ell \le B$ of the largest power $\ell^{e} \le B$. It is characterized by: for $n \neq 0$, $n \mid k(B)$ if and only if $n$ is $B$-powersmooth.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Identification with the lcm]\label{prop:lcm}
$k(B) = \lcm(1,2,\dots,B)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Write $L(B)=\lcm(1,\dots,B)$. Each $j \in [1,B]$ is $B$-powersmooth, so divides $k(B)$; hence $L(B) \mid k(B)$. Conversely $k(B)$ is a product of pairwise coprime prime powers each at most $B$, each of which is one of the integers being lcm'd, so their product divides $L(B)$. Both are nonzero, so they are equal.
\end{proof}

Proposition~\ref{prop:lcm} certifies that the object named $\lcm(1..B_1)$ in the recorded sentence is the same object as the scalar of the theory below, so the two may be compared without equivocation.

\begin{definition}[Firing]
For a finite group $G$ and $g\in G$, we say $g$ \emph{fires at bound $B$} if $g^{k(B)} = 1$, equivalently $\ord(g) \mid k(B)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cyclic model]\label{def:cyclic}
For $\Z/m$, the \emph{firing set} is $F(m,B) = \{ r \in \{0,\dots,m-1\} : m \mid r\,k(B)\}$, of cardinality $\gcd(m,k(B))$. The stage-1 \emph{success rate} is $\gcd(m,k(B))/m$. A trial on $N=pq$ is modelled as a uniform pair $(a,b) \in \Z/m_p \times \Z/m_q$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Hasse window; arithmetic ceiling]
The Hasse window of $p$ is the interval of possible group orders $[\,p+1-2\sqrt p,\ p+1+2\sqrt p\,]$. The \emph{arithmetic ceiling} is $H(p) = p + 3 + 2\lfloor \sqrt p \rfloor$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:ceil}
If $n \in \N$ and $n \le p+1+2\sqrt p$, then $n \le H(p)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\sqrt p < \lfloor \sqrt p\rfloor + 1$, so $p+1+2\sqrt p < H(p)$, and an integer strictly below $H(p)+1$ is at most $H(p)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Separated outcomes]\label{def:out}
A trial has outcome
\[
\Out(f_p,f_q) =
\begin{cases}
\dead, & f_p \wedge f_q,\\
\found\,p, & f_p \wedge \neg f_q,\\
\found\,q, & \neg f_p \wedge f_q,\\
\nothing, & \neg f_p \wedge \neg f_q,
\end{cases}
\]
where $f_p,f_q$ record firing modulo $p$, resp.\ $q$. In particular $\Out(f_p,f_q)=\dead$ \emph{iff} $f_p \wedge f_q$. The four outcomes correspond to the guarded inversion returning $\gcd = N$, $p$, $q$, $1$ respectively.
\end{definition}

\section{Size implies firing: the wall forces success}\label{sec:size}

\begin{lemma}[Size implies powersmoothness]\label{lem:size}
If $1 \le n \le B$ then $n$ is $B$-powersmooth.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Any prime power exactly dividing $n$ divides $n$, hence is at most $n \le B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Size alone fires stage 1]\label{thm:fires}
If $1 \le n \le B$ then $n \mid k(B)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:size} and Definition~\ref{def:scalar}.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:fires} is trivial and it is the entire mechanism: nothing about elliptic curves or smoothness heuristics is required, only that an integer no larger than $B$ appears among $1,\dots,B$.

\begin{theorem}[The wall forces firing]\label{thm:wallfires}
If $H(p) \le B$, $n \neq 0$ and $n \le p+1+2\sqrt p$, then $n \mid k(B)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:ceil} gives $n \le H(p) \le B$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:fires}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Universal degeneracy at the wall]\label{thm:universal}
Let $G$ be a finite group with $|G| \le p+1+2\sqrt p$ and let $B \ge H(p)$. Then $g^{k(B)}=1$ for every $g \in G$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\ord(g) \mid |G|$ and $|G| \mid k(B)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:wallfires}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Success rate one]\label{cor:rate1}
In the cyclic model with $0 < m \le B$, $F(m,B)$ is all of $\{0,\dots,m-1\}$, $\gcd(m,k(B))=m$, and the stage-1 success rate is exactly $1$.
\end{corollary}

This is the precise content of ``all curves degenerate simultaneously''. It is true. The question is what to call it.

\section{The outcome at the wall is \texorpdfstring{$\found\,p$}{found p}}\label{sec:outcome}

\begin{lemma}[Identifying the revealed gcd]\label{lem:gcd}
Let $q$ be prime and $d \mid pq$ with $p \mid d$ and $q \nmid d$. Then $d = p$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The divisors of $pq$ are $1,p,q,pq$; those divisible by $p$ are $p$ and $pq$, and $q \mid pq$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The wall outcome is $\found\,p$]\label{thm:foundp}
Let $G_p,G_q$ be finite groups with $|G_p| \le p+1+2\sqrt p$ and $|G_q| \le q+1+2\sqrt q$. Let $B \neq 0$ with $H(p) \le B$, and suppose some prime factor $r$ of $\ord(g_q)$ satisfies $r > B$. Then for all $g_p \in G_p$, $g_q \in G_q$,
\[
\Out\!\left(g_p^{k(B)}=1,\ g_q^{k(B)}=1\right) = \found\,p .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The $p$-side fires by Theorem~\ref{thm:universal}. If the $q$-side fired, $\ord(g_q)$ would be $B$-powersmooth, forcing $r \le B$, a contradiction. Apply Definition~\ref{def:out}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No death at the wall]
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:foundp} the outcome is never $\dead$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[The revealed factor]
In the situation of Theorem~\ref{thm:foundp} the guarded inversion returns a divisor $d \mid N$ with $p \mid d$, $q \nmid d$; by Lemma~\ref{lem:gcd}, $d = p$ --- a proper nontrivial factor, obtained with certainty.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Exact death rate under mod-$q$ inertness]\label{thm:deadrate}
In the cyclic model of order $m$ on the $q$-side, if $B \neq 0$ and every prime factor of $m$ exceeds $B$, then $|F(m,B)| = 1$ and the simultaneous-degeneracy rate is exactly $1/m$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\gcd(m,k(B))$ is a $B$-powersmooth divisor of $m$; each of its prime factors divides $m$ and is at most $B$, so there are none, and it equals $1$.
\end{proof}

At bit length $26$, $m \approx 2^{26}$, so the expected number of $\dead$ events over $600$ trials is below $10^{-4}$: observing zero is the prediction, not a fluke.

\section{Monotonicity: a wall is not a possible shape}\label{sec:mono}

\begin{lemma}[Divisibility chain]\label{lem:chain}
If $B \le B'$ then $k(B) \mid k(B')$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$k(B)$ is $B$-powersmooth, hence $B'$-powersmooth, hence divides $k(B')$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Firing is monotone]\label{cor:mono}
If $B \le B'$ and $n \mid k(B)$ then $n \mid k(B')$; in the cyclic model $\gcd(m,k(B)) \mid \gcd(m,k(B'))$, so $\gcd(m,k(B)) \le \gcd(m,k(B'))$ for $m>0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[No destruction wall in the firing ledger]\label{thm:nowall}
$B \mapsto |F(m,B)| = \gcd(m,k(B))$ is monotone nondecreasing; the stage-1 success rate never decreases as the bound is raised, at any scale.
\end{theorem}

A wall in the recorded sense --- success collapsing above a threshold --- is therefore not a possible shape for the firing ledger. Any observed collapse must be produced by a channel restriction (Theorem~\ref{thm:channel}) or by a labelling scheme (\S\ref{sec:ledger}).

\begin{theorem}[Sharp firing threshold]\label{thm:sharp}
For $n \neq 0$ let $M(n) = \max\{\ell^{e} : \ell^{e} \,\|\, n\}$, with $M(1)=0$. Then $n \mid k(B) \iff M(n) \le B$, and $M(n)$ is the least bound at which $n$ fires.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Prime orders]
If $n$ is prime then $n \mid k(B) \iff n \le B$: covering a prime order in the Hasse window of $p$ genuinely requires $B \gtrsim p$, so the \emph{success} threshold is real.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Cost of certainty]\label{thm:cost}
For $B \ge 1$, $2^{\pi(B)} \le k(B)$, hence $\pi(B) \le \log_2 k(B)$, and the doubling ladder for $[k(B)]P$ performs at least $\pi(B)$ doublings.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$k(B)$ is divisible by $\pi(B)$ pairwise coprime prime powers, each at least $2$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:cost} is the honest reason nobody runs ECM at $B_1 \approx p$: the wall regime is not dangerous, it is \emph{expensive}.

\section{Expected number of curves}\label{sec:expected}

\begin{definition}
For a per-curve success rate $s \in (0,1]$, the geometric expectation is $\E[T](s) = \sum_{n \ge 0}(1-s)^n = 1/s$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[{Exact $\E[T]$}]\label{thm:ET}
In the cyclic model of order $m>0$ at bound $B$, $\E[T] = m/\gcd(m,k(B))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[{$\E[T]$ is never infinite}]
$\E[T] \le m$ at every bound $B$, since $\gcd(m,k(B)) \ge 1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[{$\E[T]$ is antitone}]
$B \le B' \Rightarrow \E[T](B') \le \E[T](B)$, by Corollary~\ref{cor:mono}.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[{$\E[T] = 1$ at the wall}]\label{cor:ET1}
If $0 < m \le B$ then the success rate is $1$ and $\E[T]=1$.
\end{corollary}

These dispose of ``uncapped $\E[T]$ infinite'' comprehensively: it is false at the wall, where $\E[T]$ attains its global minimum $1$, and false at every other bound, where $\E[T] \le m$.

\section{Exact outcome counts, and the location of the real wall}\label{sec:counts}

Fix $m_p, m_q > 0$ and a scalar $k$; a trial is a pair $(a,b) \in \Z/m_p \times \Z/m_q$, so the sample space has $m_p m_q$ points. Write $g_p = \gcd(m_p,k)$, $g_q = \gcd(m_q,k)$.

\begin{definition}[Co-firing set]
$F^{c}(m,k) = \{0,\dots,m-1\}\setminus F(m,k)$, of cardinality $m - \gcd(m,k)$ for $m>0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Blocks]
$\mathrm{FP} = F(m_p,k)\times F^{c}(m_q,k)$, $\mathrm{FQ} = F^{c}(m_p,k)\times F(m_q,k)$, $\mathrm{D} = F(m_p,k)\times F(m_q,k)$, $\mathrm{N} = F^{c}(m_p,k)\times F^{c}(m_q,k)$, and the \emph{reveal set} $\mathrm{R} = \mathrm{FP}\cup\mathrm{FQ}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact block cardinalities]\label{thm:blocks}
\[
|\mathrm{D}| = g_p g_q,\quad |\mathrm{FP}| = g_p(m_q-g_q),\quad |\mathrm{FQ}| = (m_p-g_p)g_q,\quad |\mathrm{N}| = (m_p-g_p)(m_q-g_q).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Partition]
$|\mathrm{D}|+|\mathrm{FP}|+|\mathrm{FQ}|+|\mathrm{N}| = m_p m_q$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The sum factors as $(g_p + (m_p-g_p))(g_q + (m_q-g_q)) = m_p m_q$, the natural-number subtractions being exact since $g_p \le m_p$ and $g_q \le m_q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact reveal count]\label{thm:reveal}
$\mathrm{FP}$ and $\mathrm{FQ}$ are disjoint and $|\mathrm{R}| = g_p(m_q-g_q) + (m_p-g_p)g_q$.
\end{theorem}

Theorem~\ref{thm:reveal} is the master formula; everything about walls is read off from it.

\begin{theorem}[The real wall: both orders covered]\label{thm:realwall}
If $0 < m_p \le B$ and $0 < m_q \le B$ then $|\mathrm{R}| = 0$ at $k=k(B)$: every trial is $\dead$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Corollary~\ref{cor:rate1}, $g_p = m_p$ and $g_q = m_q$; substitute into Theorem~\ref{thm:reveal}.
\end{proof}

Covering both Hasse windows requires $B \gtrsim \max(p,q)$, so \emph{the destruction threshold is $\max(p,q)$}, not $\min(p,q)$.

\begin{theorem}[Wall dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
Let $|G_p|,|G_q|$ lie in their respective Hasse windows. Suppose $B \neq 0$, $H(p) \le B$, and some prime factor of $\ord(g_q)$ exceeds $B$; and suppose $H(p) \le B'$ and $H(q) \le B'$. Then the outcome at bound $B$ is $\found\,p$ and the outcome at bound $B'$ is $\dead$.
\end{theorem}

Thus $\min(p,q)$ is the \textbf{success} threshold and $\max(p,q)$ the \textbf{destruction} threshold, and between them lies a regime of guaranteed factorization.

\begin{theorem}[No wall while the $q$-side is inert]
Suppose $m_p>0$, $m_q \ge 2$, $1 \le B \le B'$, and every prime factor of $m_q$ exceeds $B'$. Then $|\mathrm{R}|$ at $B$ is at most $|\mathrm{R}|$ at $B'$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:deadrate}, $g_q=1$ at both bounds, so $|\mathrm{R}| = x(m_q-1)+(m_p-x)$ with $x = \gcd(m_p,k(\cdot))$. Since $m_q-1 \ge 1$, raising $x$ to $y \le m_p$ adds $(y-x)(m_q-1) \ge (y-x)$ and removes at most $(y-x)$. Monotonicity of $x$ in $B$ is Corollary~\ref{cor:mono}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Explicit witness of the real wall]\label{thm:witness}
With $m_p = m_q = 2$: $|\mathrm{R}| = 2$ at $B=1$ (where $k(1)=1$) and $|\mathrm{R}| = 0$ at $B=2$ (where $k(2)=2$).
\end{theorem}

This is the smallest possible genuine reveal collapse, and it occurs exactly where Theorem~\ref{thm:realwall} says it must.

\begin{theorem}[Channels are not monotone]\label{thm:channel}
With $m_p = 4$, $m_q = 6$: $|\mathrm{FP}| = 8$ at $B=2$ (where $k(2)=2$) and $|\mathrm{FP}| = 0$ at $B=3$ (where $k(3)=6$).
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
At $B=2$: $g_p = \gcd(4,2)=2$, $g_q=\gcd(6,2)=2$, so $|\mathrm{FP}| = 2\cdot 4 = 8$. At $B=3$: $g_p = \gcd(4,6)=2$, $g_q=\gcd(6,6)=6$, so $|\mathrm{FP}| = 2\cdot 0 = 0$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:channel} explains how an honest observer could see a wall. Nothing failed between $B=2$ and $B=3$: the eight trials \emph{migrated} into the $\dead$ block. The total firing count is monotone (Theorem~\ref{thm:nowall}); the per-channel counts redistribute.

\begin{theorem}[Simultaneous-degeneracy ceiling]\label{thm:omega}
For $m \neq 0$ and $B \ge 1$, $\gcd(m,k(B)) \le B^{\omega(m)}$, where $\omega(m)$ is the number of distinct prime factors of $m$; hence the death rate on a side of order $m$ is at most $B^{\omega(m)}/m$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$g = \gcd(m,k(B))$ is $B$-powersmooth with prime support inside that of $m$; writing $g$ as a product of its exact prime powers gives at most $\omega(m)$ factors, each at most $B$.
\end{proof}

\section{The experiment}\label{sec:experiment}

\subsection{Design}
Composites $N=pq$ were drawn from a bit-length-$26$, $q \gg p$ stratum ($q$ the next prime above $3p + U[1,200)$), with $40$ trials per cell on the grid
\[
B_1/p \in \{0.125,\,0.25,\,0.5,\,0.9,\,1.05\} \quad\times\quad B_2/B_1 \in \{1\ (\text{control}),\,4,\,16\},
\]
for $600$ trials. Stage 1 used the true lcm schedule with guarded affine curve operations; all four outcomes were recorded separately, with $p$-versus-$q$ separation of the revealed gcd. Hypotheses were registered before any data collection, including --- with timing disclosed --- the hypothesis that the recorded wall is a detection/accounting artifact rather than a method failure.

\subsection{Results}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Zero $\dead$ outcomes across the entire grid.}
\item Success rate $1.000$ in every cell with $B_1/p \ge 0.25$.
\item In particular $6/6$ cells at $1.000$ at $B_1/p = 0.9$ and $1.05$ --- exactly where the recorded sentence places uncapped infinite $\E[T]$.
\item The only sub-unit cells were two at $B_1/p = 0.125$, at $0.875$ and $0.95$; every miss there was $\nothing$, never $\dead$.
\item No stage-2 threshold quantity ever came into existence, so the stage-2 scaling hypotheses never armed.
\end{itemize}

Success $1.000$ above $B_1/p \approx 0.9$ is Corollary~\ref{cor:rate1} with Theorem~\ref{thm:foundp}, and misses filed as $\nothing$ rather than $\dead$ is Definition~\ref{def:out} read correctly.

\begin{remark}[A caveat on the death channel]
Theorem~\ref{thm:deadrate} gives a death rate of exactly $1/m_q$, but \emph{conditional} on mod-$q$ inertness: on every prime factor of the mod-$q$ order exceeding $B_1$. That hypothesis is not automatic merely because $q>p$; it fails whenever the mod-$q$ order happens to be $B_1$-powersmooth, an event of non-negligible probability when $q$ is only a small multiple of $B_1$. In an independent replication, at ratio $q/p \approx 3$ with $B_1 \approx p$ a few percent of trials fire on both sides and are correctly recorded as $\dead$, whereas at $q \gg B_1$ the death channel is empty as predicted. The reported zero-death count is therefore stronger than the theory alone requires, and the completeness of the death channel in the recording pipeline deserves checking in the named follow-up. The verdict is unaffected: whether a trial goes $\found\,p$ or $\dead$ is decided by the $q$-side, and raising $B_1$ never reduces the number of trials on which the $p$-side fires (Theorem~\ref{thm:nowall}).
\end{remark}

\subsection{The collision baseline}
A guarded affine implementation carries a success floor unrelated to order divisibility: roughly $1.44\,B_1$ operations each hit a vanishing denominator by accident with probability about $1/p$, giving a baseline near $1 - \exp(-1.44\,B_1/p)$.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:coll}
$1 - e^{-x} \le x$ for all real $x$.
\end{proposition}

At $B_1/p = 1/8$ this bounds the baseline by $0.18$, against an observed found-$p$ share near $0.68$: collision luck accounts for at most about a quarter of the low-edge successes. The alleged validity edge $B_1 \lesssim \min(p,q)/2$ is in any case misplaced: for $p=13$, $H(13)=22$ and the Hasse-window order $12$ has $M(12)=4$, so it fires from bound $4$ onwards, while $M(13)=13$ makes a prime order $13$ fire only from bound $13$.

\subsection{Honest disclosures}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Toy scale.} Bit length $26$. The theorems are scale-free; the grid is small. A larger-$p$ replication is the named follow-up.
\item \textbf{Stage-2 arms untested.} Stage 1 succeeded first in every cell, so the difference-stage stage-2 machinery produced zero outcomes and was effectively dead code, validated only by smoke buckets and a sign-convention check. Cross-arm differences at fixed $B_1$ are per-cell random-seed drift and must not be interpreted.
\item \textbf{Implementation route.} The coordinator was implemented inline after repeated infrastructure failures during the run.
\item \textbf{Death-channel completeness.} The theory predicts a small but nonzero death rate at ratio $q/p \approx 3$ once $B_1 \approx p$; the recorded grid contains none. Either the stratum was luckier than typical or the recorder under-counted the channel. This is flagged, not resolved.
\item \textbf{Recorder discrepancy.} A narrative summary states ``14/15 cells at 1.000'' while the canonical machine-readable record shows \emph{two} sub-unit cells, both at $B_1/p = 0.125$. The canonical record governs; the verdict is unaffected, since neither cell lies in the wall regime.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Ledger faithfulness: where the wall came from}\label{sec:ledger}

\begin{definition}[Ledger]
A \emph{ledger} is a map $L : \{0,1\}^2 \to \{\found\,p,\found\,q,\dead,\nothing\}$ from the firing pattern (fires mod $p$, fires mod $q$) to a recorded outcome.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Canonical ledger]
$L_{\mathrm{can}}(f_p,f_q) = \Out(f_p,f_q)$ of Definition~\ref{def:out}. This is the ledger the experiment used.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Faithfulness]
$L$ is \emph{faithful} if it is injective: distinct firing patterns receive distinct labels.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}
$L_{\mathrm{can}}$ is faithful.
\end{theorem}

\begin{definition}[The conflating ledger]
$L_{\mathrm{wall}}(f_p,f_q) = \dead$ if $f_p$; otherwise $\found\,q$ if $f_q$; otherwise $\nothing$. In words: \emph{any} mod-$p$ degeneracy is filed as a death.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}
$L_{\mathrm{wall}}$ is not faithful: $L_{\mathrm{wall}}(1,0) = L_{\mathrm{wall}}(1,1) = \dead$ while $(1,0)\neq(1,1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The wall sentence is the image of the conflation]\label{thm:manufacture}
On the firing pattern $(1,0)$ --- precisely the pattern produced at every trial in the regime $B \ge H(p)$ with the mod-$q$ side inert (Theorem~\ref{thm:foundp}) --- the canonical ledger records $\found\,p$ while the conflating ledger records $\dead$.
\end{theorem}

Reading $L_{\mathrm{wall}}$ over the wall regime reproduces every clause of the recorded sentence: ``every Hasse-window order divides $\lcm(1..B_1)$'' is true (Theorem~\ref{thm:wallfires}); ``all curves degenerate simultaneously'' is true (Theorem~\ref{thm:universal}); and ``uncapped $\E[T]$ infinite'' is exactly what $L_{\mathrm{wall}}$ reports, since counting every degeneracy as a loss drives the measured success rate to zero. Under $L_{\mathrm{can}}$ the same events give success rate $1$ and $\E[T]=1$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:ET1}). The wall is not a property of the elliptic curve method; it is a property of a non-injective accounting map, and the difference between the two ledgers is one arrow.

A second, independent route to the same phantom is Theorem~\ref{thm:channel}: an observer who separates $p$ from $q$ but tracks only the found-$p$ channel sees it collapse from $8$ to $0$ although the trials merely migrated.

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{A. Stage-1 scalar by the powersmooth schedule.} For each prime $\ell \le B$ take the largest $e$ with $\ell^{e}\le B$ and multiply. Cost: a sieve to $B$ plus $O(\pi(B)\log B)$ multiplications; the output has $\Theta(B)$ bits, matching $\log\lcm(1..B)\sim B$. By Proposition~\ref{prop:lcm} the result equals $\lcm(1,\dots,B)$.

\textbf{B. Exact separated block counts.} Given $m_p,m_q,B$: form $k(B)$, compute $g_p,g_q$, and return the four products of Theorem~\ref{thm:blocks}. Two gcds on $\Theta(B)$-bit numbers give exact outcome distributions with no sampling error.

\textbf{C. Faithful trial accounting.} Draw $(a,b)$ uniformly, test $m_p \mid a\,k(B)$ and $m_q \mid b\,k(B)$, emit the corresponding label of $L_{\mathrm{can}}$. Never collapse a $p$-side firing into a generic failure.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the audit does and does not overturn}
The reported mechanism is correct and genuinely interesting: there is a bound past which ECM stage 1 stops being probabilistic and becomes deterministic. What is overturned is the sign of the conclusion --- deterministic \emph{success} recorded as deterministic \emph{failure}. The parameter $\min(p,q)$ is also correct as an inflection point; it is where the success rate saturates at $1$. Only the validity edge $\min(p,q)/2$ has no correlate in the theory: firing begins far below it and never reverses above it (Theorem~\ref{thm:nowall}).

\subsection{Why the error is a natural one}
Three features conspire. In a guarded affine implementation \emph{failure is the signal}, so a defensively written exception handler files wins as losses. The wall regime produces universal degeneracy, which subjectively looks catastrophic. And the found-$p$ channel is genuinely non-monotone, so even a partially separated ledger can exhibit cliff-shaped data.

\subsection{The two-threshold picture}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lllc@{}}
\toprule
Regime & Condition & Every-trial outcome & $\E[T]$\\
\midrule
Sub-threshold & $B < M(m_p)$ & mostly $\nothing$ & $m_p/\gcd(m_p,k(B))$\\
Guaranteed-success band & $B \ge H(p)$, $q$-side inert & $\found\,p$, $\gcd=p$ & $1$\\
Genuine wall & $B \ge H(p)$ and $B \ge H(q)$ & $\dead$, $\gcd=N$ & undefined\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The middle band stretches from $p$ to $q$: narrow for balanced semiprimes, enormous for the $q\gg p$ stratum tested here. The genuine wall exists (Theorem~\ref{thm:witness}) but is unreachable in practice, since $B \gtrsim \max(p,q)$ costs more than trial division.

\subsection{Practical takeaway}
Nothing here recommends running ECM at $B_1 \approx p$: Theorem~\ref{thm:cost} makes that exponentially expensive. What is established is that the reason to avoid the regime is \emph{cost}, not \emph{danger}, and that any implementation reporting failures there is misreporting. The corrective is cheap: separate the four outcomes and record which prime the gcd revealed.

\section{Future work}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Larger-$p$ replication with per-operation tracing}, distinguishing order-divisibility hits from random-collision hits at each ladder step well beyond bit length $26$.
\item \textbf{Exercising the stage-2 arms}, via a grid where stage 1 is deliberately starved so that the stage-2 scaling hypotheses actually arm.
\item \textbf{Balanced semiprimes}, where the guaranteed-success band narrows and the $\dead$ block becomes reachable in principle; Theorem~\ref{thm:omega} bounds its size and could be sharpened.
\item \textbf{Beyond the cyclic model}: extending the exact block counts to general finite abelian orders $\Z/d \times \Z/e$.
\item \textbf{Ledger faithfulness as a general audit criterion}: any pipeline compressing a multi-state ground truth into fewer recorded labels admits the same failure mode.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

The recorded ECM self-destruction wall does not exist as described. Above $B_1 \ge p+1+2\sqrt p$, every Hasse-window order divides $\lcm(1,\dots,B_1)$ --- exactly as recorded --- but this makes every curve \emph{succeed}, not die: the guarded inversion returns $\gcd = p$ with certainty, the per-curve success rate is $1$, and the expected number of curves is $1$. The stage-1 success rate is monotone in the bound, so a wall is not a possible shape; the expected number of curves is bounded by the group order at every bound, so it is never infinite. The genuine destruction regime requires both Hasse windows covered and therefore sits at $\max(p,q)$, with $\min(p,q)$ the success threshold. The recorded sentence is exactly what a single non-injective step in an outcome ledger produces. Six hundred outcome-separated trials across and beyond the alleged wall recorded zero deaths and perfect success in every cell at and above the claimed cliff.

When a method's success is signalled by an exception, the accounting must ask not merely \emph{whether} the computation broke, but \emph{where}.

